// Spoken answers (60-90 s) for database_performance guide
(window.SPOKEN = window.SPOKEN || {})['database_performance'] = {
  1: {
    a: `An index is a separate data structure that lets the database find rows without scanning the whole table. The default type in most relational databases is the B-tree, a balanced tree where each node is a page holding sorted keys and pointers. The root points to internal nodes, which point to leaf nodes; in a B+ tree variant, all entries live in the leaves, which are linked together in order. A lookup starts at the root, compares the search key to keys in the page to pick the right child, and descends a few levels, usually three or four even for hundreds of millions of rows, because each page holds hundreds of entries. That makes lookups logarithmic. The leaf entry gives the row location, such as a heap tuple pointer in PostgreSQL or the primary key in an InnoDB secondary index. Because leaves are sorted and linked, B-trees also support range scans, ordering, and prefix matching efficiently. Inserts find the right leaf and add an entry; when a page is full, it splits, which keeps the tree balanced but can fragment it. The costs: indexes use disk and memory, and slow writes since every insert, update, or delete must maintain them. So index columns used in filters, joins, and sorts, and don't over-index.`,
    f: ["Why are B-tree lookups logarithmic?","What does an index cost on writes?"]
  },
  2: {
    a: `Several situations cause the optimiser to ignore an existing index. A function or expression on the column, such as WHERE LOWER(email) = 'x' or WHERE date(created_at) = ..., prevents using a plain index; fix it by rewriting the predicate or creating an expression index. Implicit type conversions, like comparing a string column to a number, defeat the index. A leading wildcard, LIKE '%abc', can't use a B-tree because it can't seek by prefix. For composite indexes, skipping the leftmost column means the index isn't usable. Low selectivity: if the query returns a large fraction of the table, say more than ten to twenty percent, a sequential scan is cheaper than many random lookups, so the planner rightly chooses it. Stale statistics lead to wrong row estimates and bad plans; run ANALYZE. Small tables are scanned anyway. OR conditions across different columns, negations like != or NOT IN, and large IN lists can prevent index use. Different collation or data types in joins break matching. Functions in ORDER BY, or ordering that doesn't match the index, force sorts. Also, parameter sniffing in some databases and planner cost settings such as random_page_cost. Diagnose with EXPLAIN, check statistics and the query's predicates, and test whether forcing the index actually helps before assuming the planner is wrong.`,
    f: ["Why might a sequential scan beat an index?","How do expression indexes help?"]
  },
  3: {
    a: `A composite index covers several columns in a defined order, and it behaves like a phone book sorted by last name, then first name. The index is sorted by the first column, then by the second within equal first values, and so on. Therefore column order determines which queries it can serve. It supports equality or range on the leftmost prefix: an index on (a, b, c) helps queries filtering on a; on a and b; on a, b, and c; but not on b alone or c alone, and only partially on a and c. A range condition on a column stops later columns from being used for seeking; so equality columns should come before the range column. Rules of thumb: put equality-filtered columns first, then the range or sort column; among equality columns, put the most selective first, although query patterns matter more than selectivity; and put columns that serve ORDER BY in the right place so the sort can be avoided. One composite index can replace several single-column ones, saving write overhead, and it can be a covering index if it includes all needed columns. Avoid redundant indexes: (a, b) makes a separate index on (a) unnecessary.`,
    f: ["Why should equality columns come before range columns?","Does an index on (a, b) help a query on b alone?"]
  },
  4: {
    a: `A covering index contains all the columns a query needs, so the database can answer it from the index alone without visiting the table. In PostgreSQL this appears as an Index Only Scan, and in MySQL as Using index in EXPLAIN. It avoids the extra random I/O of fetching rows from the heap or clustered index, which can speed up queries significantly, especially for large tables or queries returning many rows. You build one by including the filtered and selected columns in the index. In PostgreSQL 11 and later, the INCLUDE clause adds non-key payload columns that are stored in the leaf entries but not used for ordering or uniqueness, keeping the key small: for example CREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id, created_at) INCLUDE (total). In MySQL InnoDB, secondary indexes automatically contain the primary key, so queries needing only indexed columns plus the primary key are covered. Caveats: wide indexes use more space and slow writes; in PostgreSQL, index-only scans rely on the visibility map, so a table with many recent updates or insufficient vacuuming still must check the heap; covering everything for every query defeats the purpose, so reserve it for hot, frequent queries. Verify with EXPLAIN that heap fetches are low, and compare the benefits to the write overhead.`,
    f: ["What does INCLUDE do in PostgreSQL?","Why does vacuum matter for index-only scans?"]
  },
  5: {
    a: `Index bloat is wasted space inside an index: pages that are mostly empty or filled with dead entries. It arises because updates and deletes in MVCC databases leave dead tuples, and index entries pointing to them remain until vacuum cleans them; pages may then be only partly reused, and B-tree pages are rarely merged, so the index grows larger than the live data needs. Heavy update or delete workloads, long-running transactions that block vacuum cleanup, and random key insertion patterns worsen it. Effects: larger indexes mean more disk and fewer pages fitting in memory, slower scans, and more I/O. Detection in PostgreSQL: compare index size against expected size, use the pgstattuple extension's pgstatindex for leaf density and fragmentation, use bloat estimation queries, or monitor index size growth over time. In MySQL, check data_free and fragmentation in information_schema. Fixes: REINDEX rebuilds the index, and REINDEX CONCURRENTLY in PostgreSQL 12 and later does so without blocking writes; pg_repack can rebuild tables and indexes online; in MySQL, OPTIMIZE TABLE or ALTER TABLE rebuilds. Prevention: tune autovacuum to run frequently on busy tables, avoid long transactions, use HOT updates by leaving fillfactor free and not indexing frequently changing columns, drop unused indexes, and monitor bloat regularly.`,
    f: ["How do long transactions cause bloat?","How do you rebuild an index without blocking writes?"]
  },
  6: {
    a: `EXPLAIN shows the plan the optimiser chose, and EXPLAIN ANALYZE actually runs the query and adds real timings and row counts, so you can compare estimates to reality. The plan is a tree; read it from the innermost, most indented nodes outward, since data flows upward. Each node shows the operation, such as Seq Scan, Index Scan, Index Only Scan, Bitmap Heap Scan, Nested Loop, Hash Join, Merge Join, Sort, or Aggregate, with estimated cost, estimated rows, and with ANALYZE the actual time, actual rows, and loops. Key things I look for: large gaps between estimated and actual rows, which signal stale statistics or correlated columns and often explain bad plan choices; sequential scans on big tables where an index should apply; expensive sorts that spill to disk; nested loops with high loop counts; the node where most actual time is spent, remembering to multiply by loops; filters removing many rows after the scan, shown as rows removed by filter, suggesting a missing or better index; and in PostgreSQL, using BUFFERS to see cache hits versus disk reads. Remember that ANALYZE executes the statement, so wrap data-modifying queries in a transaction and roll back. Then act: add or adjust indexes, rewrite the query, update statistics, or change settings, and re-measure.`,
    f: ["Why compare estimated and actual rows?","Why wrap EXPLAIN ANALYZE of an UPDATE in a transaction?"]
  },
  7: {
    a: `The N+1 query problem happens when code runs one query to fetch a list of N parent records, then runs an additional query per parent to fetch related data, resulting in N+1 round trips. For example, loading 100 orders and then querying each order's customer separately gives 101 queries. It often comes from ORMs with lazy loading, where accessing a relationship in a loop triggers a hidden query. Each query is fast, so it's invisible in slow query logs, but the total latency and database load multiply with the data size, and network round trips dominate. Detection: enable query logging or counting in development and tests; use APM and tracing tools showing many repeated similar queries within one request; look at pg_stat_statements for queries with huge call counts; and libraries such as Bullet for Rails or django-debug-toolbar, or assertions on query counts in tests. Fixes: eager loading, such as select_related or prefetch_related in Django, includes in Rails, or JOIN FETCH in JPA, which uses a join or one extra query with an IN list for all parents; write an explicit join; batch lookups with a data loader pattern, common in GraphQL; or cache lookups. Be careful that eager loading everything can fetch too much data, and large joins can duplicate rows.`,
    f: ["Why does N+1 not show up in the slow query log?","How does a data loader solve it in GraphQL?"]
  },
  8: {
    a: `When a query was fast and became slow, something changed, so I look for what. Start by measuring: is it slow now consistently, or intermittently, for everyone or specific parameters? Compare the current EXPLAIN ANALYZE against the old plan if available, from logs or auto_explain. The commonest causes: data growth, where a table crossed a size or selectivity threshold; stale or inaccurate statistics, causing the planner to choose a poor plan, so run ANALYZE; a plan flip, for example from index scan to sequential scan or a different join order; bloat from a lack of vacuuming; a dropped or invalid index, or an index that changed after a migration; changes in the query or the ORM, a new deployment, or different parameter values hitting skewed data. Resource contention: locks and blocked sessions, long-running transactions, connection saturation, CPU, memory pressure causing cache evictions, disk I/O saturation, or noisy neighbours on shared infrastructure; checkpoints and autovacuum activity at the same time. Also configuration changes, version upgrades, and failover to a replica with a cold cache. Use pg_stat_statements to see when mean time changed, check locks in pg_locks and pg_stat_activity, and review monitoring dashboards and deployment history. Fix the cause, then add alerting on query latency regression.`,
    f: ["What is a plan flip?","How do you compare with the old plan?"]
  },
  9: {
    a: `Window functions compute a value for each row using a set of related rows, defined by OVER with optional PARTITION BY, ORDER BY, and a frame, without collapsing rows as GROUP BY does. Examples: ROW_NUMBER, RANK, and DENSE_RANK for ranking; LAG and LEAD to access previous and next rows; running totals and moving averages with SUM or AVG over a frame; FIRST_VALUE and LAST_VALUE; and NTILE for buckets. They outperform alternatives for several reasons. Compared to correlated subqueries, which may re-execute for each row, a window function makes a single pass over the data, after one sort or hash by the partition. Compared to self-joins, such as joining a table to itself to get the previous row or a running total, which can be quadratic, windows avoid the join and read the table once. They're ideal for top N per group, using ROW_NUMBER filtered in an outer query; calculating differences between consecutive rows; percentage of total; running balances; and deduplication keeping the latest row per key.`,
    f: ["How do you get the top N rows per group?","Why can a window function beat a correlated subquery?"]
  },
  10: {
    a: `To optimise a slow join, I start with EXPLAIN ANALYZE to see the join algorithm, row estimates, and where time goes. Strategies. Indexes: make sure the join columns are indexed on the side being looked up, especially foreign keys, and that data types and collations match on both sides so indexes are usable. Reduce the input before joining: filter early with WHERE conditions on each table, select only needed columns, and use subqueries or CTEs to pre-aggregate large tables before joining. Choose the right join method: nested loop with an index suits small outer sets; hash join suits large unsorted inputs and needs enough work_mem to avoid spilling; merge join suits inputs already sorted by the join key. Fix bad row estimates by updating statistics, increasing the statistics target, or creating extended statistics for correlated columns, since misestimates lead to the wrong algorithm or join order. Check join order and the number of tables joined; in complex queries, break them into steps. Avoid joining on expressions, and avoid unnecessary joins or DISTINCT used to hide duplication from many-to-many joins; use EXISTS for semi-joins instead of joining just to filter. Consider denormalisation, materialised views, or pre-computed aggregates for hot reporting queries. Partition large tables so that partition pruning limits the data.`,
    f: ["When is a hash join better than a nested loop?","Why can bad row estimates ruin a join?"]
  },
  11: {
    a: `PostgreSQL uses MVCC, so an update or delete doesn't remove the old row version; it marks it dead, and readers with older snapshots may still need it. VACUUM reclaims space from dead tuples so it can be reused, updates the visibility map, which enables index-only scans, updates the free space map, and freezes old transaction IDs to prevent transaction ID wraparound, which would otherwise force the database to stop accepting writes to protect data. Without vacuuming, tables and indexes bloat, scans slow down, disk fills, and eventually wraparound becomes an emergency. Plain VACUUM runs concurrently with normal operations and makes space reusable within the table but typically doesn't shrink the file; VACUUM FULL rewrites the table to return space to the OS but takes an exclusive lock, so avoid it on busy tables, using pg_repack as an alternative. Autovacuum runs it automatically, triggered when dead tuples exceed a threshold plus a scale factor of the table size; for large, busy tables, the default scale factor of twenty percent is too lax, so tune per table with lower thresholds, higher cost limits, and enough workers. ANALYZE, often run along with it, updates planner statistics. Common problems are long-running transactions or abandoned replication slots, which prevent cleanup of dead rows.`,
    f: ["What is transaction ID wraparound?","Why avoid VACUUM FULL on a busy table?"]
  },
  12: {
    a: `In PostgreSQL, pg_stat_activity shows current sessions: query text, state, start times, wait events, and which backend is blocking which. To find long-running queries, filter where state is active and now() minus query_start exceeds a threshold, and also look for sessions idle in transaction, which can hold locks and block vacuum. Before killing, understand what the query is: who runs it, whether it's blocking others, found via pg_blocking_pids or the pg_locks views, and whether it's part of an important job, because killing a long write transaction triggers a rollback that may itself take a long time. The graceful action is pg_cancel_backend(pid), which sends a cancel to the current query and leaves the connection open. If that doesn't work, pg_terminate_backend(pid) terminates the whole session. Avoid killing the postmaster or using operating system kill -9, which forces a crash recovery restart. Prevention is better: set statement_timeout to cap query runtime, idle_in_transaction_session_timeout to drop abandoned transactions, and lock_timeout to avoid long waits on locks; use role-level or application-level settings. Log slow queries with log_min_duration_statement, and use auto_explain for plans. Then investigate why it was slow, adding indexes or rewriting. In MySQL the equivalents are SHOW PROCESSLIST and KILL QUERY or KILL connection id.`,
    f: ["What is the difference between cancel and terminate?","Which timeouts prevent runaway queries?"]
  },
  13: {
    a: `pg_stat_statements is a PostgreSQL extension that tracks execution statistics for every distinct normalised query, with constants replaced by parameters: number of calls, total, mean, min, max and standard deviation of execution time, rows returned, shared buffer hits and reads, temporary file usage, WAL generated, and more. Enable it by adding it to shared_preload_libraries, restarting, and running CREATE EXTENSION pg_stat_statements. To find the worst queries, sort in different ways, because the worst depends on the question. By total_exec_time to find where the database spends most time overall, usually the best starting point, since a moderately slow query called millions of times matters more than a rare slow one. By mean_exec_time for individually slow queries. By calls to spot chatty patterns like N+1. By rows for large result sets. By shared_blks_read for I/O-heavy queries, and by temp_blks_written for queries spilling to disk. Also look at stddev for inconsistent performance. Then take the top candidates, run EXPLAIN ANALYZE with realistic parameters, and optimise. Reset the statistics with pg_stat_statements_reset after a change to measure the impact, or take periodic snapshots to track trends. Combine with the slow query log, and use dashboards from tools like pgwatch or monitoring agents. Watch the query text length and the memory overhead.`,
    f: ["Why sort by total time rather than mean time?","How do you measure the impact of a fix?"]
  },
  14: {
    a: `PostgreSQL memory tuning centres on a few parameters. shared_buffers is PostgreSQL's own cache of data pages; a common starting point is around twenty-five percent of RAM, as the operating system's page cache also helps, with more only if testing shows benefit. work_mem is the memory per sort or hash operation, per query node, per connection, so total usage can be many multiples; raise it carefully, globally to modest values, or per session or role for heavy reports, since too high a value across many connections causes out-of-memory kills, while too low causes spills to disk, visible in EXPLAIN as external sorts or temp files. maintenance_work_mem is used for VACUUM, CREATE INDEX, and ALTER TABLE, and can be much higher, a few hundred megabytes to gigabytes. effective_cache_size is not an allocation but a hint to the planner about how much memory is available for caching in total, including the OS cache, typically fifty to seventy-five percent of RAM, influencing index versus sequential scan decisions. Others: wal_buffers, huge_pages to reduce page table overhead, and max_connections, which multiplies per-connection memory, so use a pooler instead of raising it. Also keep an eye on overcommit settings in Linux to avoid the OOM killer.`,
    f: ["Why is work_mem dangerous to raise globally?","What does effective_cache_size actually do?"]
  },
  15: {
    a: `The InnoDB buffer pool is MySQL's main memory cache for table data and index pages. Reads check the pool first; a miss reads the page from disk into the pool. Writes modify pages in memory, marking them dirty, and they're flushed to disk later in the background, while durability comes from the redo log. The pool is managed with a modified LRU list, divided into a young sublist and an old sublist, so a large table scan doesn't evict the hot working set: new pages enter at the midpoint. Tuning. innodb_buffer_pool_size is the most important setting: on a dedicated database server, typically sixty to eighty percent of RAM, sized to hold the working set, leaving room for the operating system, connections, and other buffers. Check effectiveness with the buffer pool hit ratio from Innodb_buffer_pool_reads versus read requests, though what matters more is whether disk reads hurt latency. Use multiple buffer pool instances for concurrency on large pools. Resize online in recent versions in chunks. Enable the dump and load at shutdown and startup, so the cache is warm after a restart. Tune flushing with innodb_io_capacity to match the storage's IOPS, and the redo log size for write-heavy workloads, as a small log forces frequent flushing. Monitor free pages, dirty page percentage, and evictions.`,
    f: ["Why does the buffer pool use a midpoint insertion?","How big should the buffer pool be?"]
  },
  16: {
    a: `The MySQL slow query log records statements that take longer than long_query_time seconds, optionally also those not using indexes, with details such as query time, lock time, rows sent, and rows examined. Enable it with slow_query_log on, set the threshold, for instance one second at first and lower later, such as 100 milliseconds, set the log file or table output, and optionally log_queries_not_using_indexes with a rate limit to avoid noise. In production, enable it carefully and rotate logs. To analyse, don't read it by hand; aggregate with pt-query-digest from Percona Toolkit, or mysqldumpslow, which groups similar queries and ranks them by total time, calls, and average. What I look for: queries with a high total time, which is the best optimisation target; a large ratio of rows examined to rows sent, indicating missing or ineffective indexes, since the query scans many rows to return few; high lock time, suggesting contention; queries creating temporary tables on disk or sorting with filesort; and sudden new queries after a deployment. Next, run EXPLAIN on the top offenders, add or adjust indexes, rewrite queries, and measure again. Performance Schema and sys schema views provide similar information with less overhead. Review the log regularly, not only during incidents, and combine it with application tracing.`,
    f: ["What does a high rows examined to rows sent ratio mean?","Why aggregate with pt-query-digest?"]
  },
  17: {
    a: `MySQL replication lag is the delay between a transaction committing on the primary and being applied on a replica, which causes stale reads. Detect it with SHOW REPLICA STATUS, looking at Seconds_Behind_Source, though this metric can be misleading, for instance showing zero when the I/O thread is stalled; better to use a heartbeat table, as pt-heartbeat does, which writes timestamps on the primary and compares on the replica for accurate measurement. Compare the I/O thread, which fetches binary logs, and the SQL thread, which applies them, to see where it's lagging. Causes: single-threaded apply of a high write volume, since historically the replica applied transactions serially; large transactions or bulk updates and deletes that take a long time to apply; weaker replica hardware or disk I/O contention from heavy reads; missing indexes on the replica for row-based updates, leading to full table scans per row; long-running queries or locks on the replica; network latency; and DDL operations. Reduce lag: enable multi-threaded replication with replica_parallel_workers and logical-clock parallelism; break large batch operations into small chunks; ensure primary keys exist on all tables; use faster storage and adequate hardware; tune durability settings on replicas carefully; move heavy reporting elsewhere; and route latency-sensitive reads to the primary or use semi-synchronous replication. Alert on lag thresholds.`,
    f: ["Why is Seconds_Behind_Source sometimes misleading?","How does a missing primary key worsen lag?"]
  },
  18: {
    a: `Connection pooling maintains a set of reusable database connections that applications borrow and return, instead of opening a new connection for each request. It's essential because connections are expensive: establishing one involves a TCP and TLS handshake and authentication, and in PostgreSQL each connection is a separate process using several megabytes of memory; maintaining thousands of them harms performance through memory use, context switching, and lock contention. Databases also have a hard max_connections limit. A pool bounds the number of connections, so the database operates at a healthy concurrency level, typically a small multiple of CPU cores, while many more application requests queue for a connection. It reduces latency by eliminating connection setup. Types: in-application pools, such as HikariCP, which is per process; and external poolers, like PgBouncer or ProxySQL, or RDS Proxy and Cloud SQL managed pooling, which multiplex many client connections onto fewer server connections and matter when there are many application instances, as in Kubernetes or serverless. PgBouncer modes: session, transaction, and statement; transaction mode gives the best multiplexing but breaks session-level features such as prepared statements in some setups, advisory locks, and temporary tables. Size pools carefully: bigger isn't better, set timeouts, validate connections, and monitor pool wait times and usage.`,
    f: ["Why doesn't a bigger pool mean better performance?","What breaks in PgBouncer transaction mode?"]
  },
  19: {
    a: `When a database runs out of connections, applications see errors like too many connections. First stabilise: identify who holds the connections. In PostgreSQL, query pg_stat_activity grouped by application, user, client address, and state; in MySQL, SHOW PROCESSLIST. Look for lots of idle connections, which suggests oversized pools or leaks; idle in transaction sessions, where the application opened a transaction and never finished; many active sessions all waiting on a lock or a slow query, causing requests to pile up and take more connections; or a sudden traffic spike or retry storm. Immediate relief: terminate idle-in-transaction or runaway sessions, kill the blocking query, restart or scale down the misbehaving application, and temporarily raise the limit if memory allows, though that's only a stopgap. Root causes and fixes: connection leaks in code, fixed by always releasing connections, using context managers, and setting max lifetime and leak detection; oversized pools times many replicas; missing pooling, where each request opens a connection; slow queries holding connections longer; and lack of timeouts. Long-term: add an external pooler like PgBouncer, right-size pools so total across instances stays below the limit, set idle_in_transaction_session_timeout and statement_timeout, add retry with backoff and circuit breakers, and alert on connection usage percentage before exhaustion.`,
    f: ["How do you find leaked connections?","What is the danger of retry storms?"]
  },
  20: {
    a: `A deadlock happens when two or more transactions each hold a lock the other needs, so none can proceed. For example, transaction A locks row 1 then wants row 2, while transaction B locked row 2 and wants row 1. The database detects the cycle, using a wait-for graph, picks a victim, aborts it with an error such as PostgreSQL's deadlock detected or MySQL's error 1213, and the other continues. Detection: check the database logs, where PostgreSQL logs the involved queries after deadlock_timeout, and in MySQL use SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS to see the latest deadlock, or enable innodb_print_all_deadlocks. Monitor deadlock counts in metrics. Prevention: access resources in a consistent order across all code paths, for example always update rows sorted by ID; keep transactions short and avoid user interaction or network calls inside them; lock only what's needed, acquiring locks early with SELECT FOR UPDATE where appropriate, possibly with NOWAIT or SKIP LOCKED for queues; use appropriate isolation levels; add indexes so updates lock fewer rows, since without indexes InnoDB may lock many rows or gaps; and avoid large batch updates in one transaction by chunking. Recovery: applications must catch the deadlock error and retry the transaction, with backoff, since the aborted transaction rolled back completely. Make operations idempotent.`,
    f: ["Why must the application retry after a deadlock?","How does consistent lock ordering prevent deadlocks?"]
  },
  21: {
    a: `The Write-Ahead Log is PostgreSQL's mechanism for durability and recovery: before any change to data pages is written to disk, a record describing the change is appended and flushed to the WAL. On commit, only the WAL needs to be flushed, which is sequential and fast, while the dirty data pages are written later at checkpoints. After a crash, PostgreSQL replays the WAL from the last checkpoint to restore consistency. The WAL also underpins replication: in streaming replication, a standby connects to the primary and receives WAL records in real time, then replays them to stay in sync, which is physical replication of the byte-level changes. Replication slots make sure the primary keeps WAL until the standby has consumed it, though an abandoned slot makes WAL accumulate and can fill the disk. For point-in-time recovery, you take a base backup, a consistent copy of the data directory, and continuously archive WAL segments to durable storage with archive_command or tools like pgBackRest and WAL-G. To restore, you copy back the base backup, then replay archived WAL up to a chosen target time, transaction ID, or named restore point, recovering the database to a moment just before an accidental delete or bad deploy. wal_level controls the detail, and logical replication needs wal_level logical.`,
    f: ["Why can an abandoned replication slot fill the disk?","What do you need for point-in-time recovery?"]
  },
  22: {
    a: `In synchronous replication, the primary waits for one or more replicas to confirm they have received, or applied, the transaction before acknowledging the commit to the client. That gives zero data loss on primary failure, an RPO of zero, because committed data exists on at least two nodes, at the cost of higher commit latency, bounded by the replica round trip, and an availability risk: if the synchronous replica goes down or becomes unreachable, writes stall unless you configure fallback, such as quorum commit with multiple candidates. In asynchronous replication, the primary commits locally and sends changes to replicas afterwards, without waiting. It offers the lowest write latency, no dependence on replica health, and works well across regions, but replicas lag, so reads from them can be stale, and if the primary fails, recent transactions that hadn't been shipped can be lost. Choose synchronous for financial or critical data where losing committed transactions is unacceptable, and where replicas are close, in the same region or nearby zones. Choose asynchronous for read scaling, cross-region disaster recovery, and workloads where small potential data loss is tolerable and latency matters. Intermediate options: semi-synchronous replication in MySQL, PostgreSQL's remote_write versus remote_apply levels, per-transaction synchronous_commit settings, and quorum-based configurations. Align the choice with the business RPO and RTO.`,
    f: ["What is the availability risk of synchronous replication?","How can you make durability a per-transaction choice?"]
  },
  23: {
    a: `A lagging read replica means it's applying changes slower than the primary generates them. Start by measuring what kind of lag it is: receive lag, where WAL or binlog isn't arriving fast enough, versus replay lag, where it's arriving but not applied quickly. In PostgreSQL, compare pg_stat_replication sent, write, flush, and replay positions on the primary; in MySQL, compare I/O and SQL thread status. Causes: write bursts or large batch jobs and big transactions that take long to replay; a single-threaded replay unable to keep up with highly concurrent writes; replica hardware or storage weaker than the primary; heavy read queries on the replica, which in PostgreSQL can conflict with replay, causing it to wait or cancel queries, controlled by max_standby_streaming_delay and hot_standby_feedback; missing indexes or primary keys on the replica; network bandwidth or latency, especially cross-region; vacuum or checkpoint activity; and long locks. Fixes: provision replicas with equal or better resources and fast disks; enable parallel apply where supported; break large writes into smaller batches; move heavy analytics to a dedicated replica; add the right indexes; tune the conflict-handling settings knowingly; improve network paths and compression; and add more replicas to spread read load. For consistency, route reads that need fresh data to the primary, or use read-your-writes techniques.`,
    f: ["How can replica queries conflict with replay in PostgreSQL?","How do you handle reads needing fresh data?"]
  },
  24: {
    a: `Schema design determines how much data the database must touch, how efficiently it can use indexes, and how well it scales. Key decisions: normalisation level, which reduces redundancy and update anomalies but needs joins, versus selective denormalisation for read-heavy paths. Data types: choose the smallest appropriate types, since smaller rows mean more rows per page, better cache use, and smaller indexes; use proper types like integers for IDs, timestamps instead of strings, and numeric versus floating point for money; avoid oversized text columns in hot tables, and be careful with JSON blobs that can't be indexed or constrained as easily. Primary keys: sequential integers or time-ordered IDs keep inserts efficient and indexes compact, while random UUIDs cause page splits and fragmentation, particularly in InnoDB where the primary key is the clustered index. Indexes aligned to access patterns, with foreign keys indexed. Constraints, such as NOT NULL, unique, and foreign keys, which help the optimiser and integrity. Partitioning for large time-based or tenant-based tables, aiding pruning and retention. Avoiding wide tables with many rarely used columns, and moving large or infrequently used data elsewhere. Handling of NULLs, and soft deletes that bloat tables and complicate indexes. Design for the query workload, not just the entities; consider read versus write ratios, growth, and tenancy.`,
    f: ["Why do random UUID primary keys hurt InnoDB?","How do data types affect performance?"]
  },
  25: {
    a: `Table partitioning splits one large logical table into smaller physical pieces, called partitions, by a key. Methods: range, commonly by date; list, by discrete values such as region or tenant; and hash, to spread rows evenly. In PostgreSQL, declarative partitioning handles this natively. Benefits: partition pruning, where queries filtering on the partition key scan only relevant partitions; cheap data retention, since dropping or detaching an old partition is instant compared with a huge DELETE that bloats the table and needs vacuuming; smaller indexes per partition that fit memory better; faster maintenance, such as vacuum and reindex per partition; and the ability to put partitions on different storage. Use it for very large tables, hundreds of gigabytes or more, time-series or log-like data with retention policies, and multi-tenant data. Pitfalls: queries that don't filter on the partition key scan all partitions and can be slower than a single table; primary keys and unique constraints must include the partition key; too many partitions increase planning time and memory use; foreign keys to and from partitioned tables have restrictions; a skewed key creates hot partitions; creating future partitions must be automated, using pg_partman or scheduled jobs, otherwise inserts fail; and migrating an existing table to partitioned form needs planning.`,
    f: ["Why must unique keys include the partition key?","How does partitioning make retention cheap?"]
  },
  26: {
    a: `Normalisation organises data so each fact is stored once, removing redundancy and update anomalies, typically up to third normal form: every non-key column depends on the key, the whole key, and nothing but the key. Benefits: data integrity, smaller storage, easier writes, since you update one place. The cost is that reads need joins across tables. Denormalisation deliberately duplicates or precomputes data to make reads faster and simpler, such as storing a customer name on an order, keeping counters, or flattening tables for reporting. Benefits: fewer joins, lower read latency; costs: duplicated data to keep consistent, more complex writes, and risk of inconsistency. In practice, I start normalised, because it's correct and flexible, and measure. Denormalise only where profiling shows a join or aggregation is a real bottleneck, and where reads greatly outnumber writes. Good techniques include materialised views, summary or rollup tables updated by triggers or jobs, cached columns such as order totals, and read models separate from the write model, as in CQRS. Analytics systems often use star schemas, which are intentionally denormalised. Keep the source of truth normalised, and make the duplicate data derivable and rebuildable, with the update logic in one place, such as in triggers or a defined pipeline.`,
    f: ["How do you keep denormalised data consistent?","When is a star schema appropriate?"]
  },
  27: {
    a: `I choose NoSQL when the data model, scale, or access pattern doesn't fit a relational database well, not because relational can't scale; modern relational systems scale very far. Relational is the default when you need complex queries and joins, strong transactional guarantees across multiple entities, flexible ad hoc querying, and a well-understood schema. NoSQL families suit specific cases. Key-value stores like Redis or DynamoDB suit very high-throughput lookups by key, caches, sessions, and counters, with predictable low latency at scale. Document stores such as MongoDB or Firestore suit hierarchical, evolving, or varied records read as a unit, like catalogues or profiles, where schema flexibility helps. Wide-column stores like Bigtable or Cassandra suit massive write throughput and time-series or event data at petabyte scale with known access patterns. Graph databases suit highly connected data and relationship traversals, like social networks or fraud detection. Search engines handle full-text search. Trade-offs to weigh: weaker or limited transactions and joins, often needing denormalisation and designing the data model around known queries, eventual consistency in some systems, less flexible querying, and operational differences. Consider the team's skills, managed service availability, consistency requirements, and the cost of getting it wrong. Many systems use polyglot persistence: a relational database as the system of record, with specialised stores for caching, search, or analytics.`,
    f: ["Why isn't scale alone a reason to leave relational?","What is polyglot persistence?"]
  },
  28: {
    a: `Production Redis needs attention to performance, memory, persistence, and availability. Performance: Redis is single-threaded for command execution, so avoid slow commands like KEYS, large SMEMBERS, or big range operations; use SCAN instead, and watch the slowlog and latency monitoring. Use pipelining to cut round trips, and connection pooling. Avoid big keys and hot keys. Memory: set maxmemory and an eviction policy that fits the use, such as allkeys-lru for caches, or noeviction for primary data stores; set TTLs on cache keys; watch fragmentation ratio, and use memory-efficient data structures and encodings. Leave headroom for fork copy-on-write during snapshots. Persistence: RDB snapshots give compact point-in-time backups but can lose recent writes; AOF logs every write with fsync options like everysec, offering better durability at some cost; use both for important data, and put persistence on suitable disks. Availability: use replicas with Redis Sentinel for automatic failover, or Redis Cluster for sharding and scale; managed services like ElastiCache or Memorystore handle much of this. Be aware of replication being asynchronous, so failover can lose writes. Security: require authentication, ACLs, TLS, and keep it off the public internet. Operations: disable transparent huge pages, tune the OS settings, monitor memory, evictions, hit rate, connected clients, replication lag, and latency, and plan capacity.`,
    f: ["Why avoid the KEYS command in production?","What is the trade-off between RDB and AOF?"]
  },
  29: {
    a: `Bigtable, and its open-source cousin HBase, is a wide-column NoSQL store: a sparse, distributed, sorted map. Data is organised in rows identified by a row key, with columns grouped into column families, and each cell can hold multiple timestamped versions. Rows are stored sorted lexicographically by row key and split into tablets spread across nodes, giving very high write and read throughput with single-digit millisecond latency at huge scale. It has no joins or secondary indexes, and offers atomic operations only at the single-row level. It's the right choice for very large datasets, terabytes to petabytes, with high-throughput writes and known access patterns: time-series and IoT data, monitoring metrics, event and clickstream data, financial ticks, personalisation, and analytics serving. Data modelling revolves around the row key, since it determines both locality and performance. Design the key for the main query pattern: lookups by prefix or range scans; include the entity ID and then a timestamp for time-series. Avoid hotspots from sequential keys, such as a timestamp prefix, which sends all writes to one node; use field promotion, salting, or hashing, or reversed timestamps for most-recent-first scans. Keep the number of column families small, put data accessed together in the same row, and keep rows reasonably sized.`,
    f: ["Why do sequential row keys cause hotspots?","What is field promotion?"]
  },
  30: {
    a: `Tuning Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL combines instance sizing, database flags, and good practices. Sizing: choose machine type with enough memory for the working set, SSD storage with enough capacity because IOPS and throughput scale with disk size, and enable automatic storage increase. Availability: enable the high availability configuration with a standby in another zone, and read replicas for scaling reads or cross-region disaster recovery. Configuration through database flags: tune shared_buffers, work_mem, maintenance_work_mem, and effective_cache_size sensibly, noting that Cloud SQL sets reasonable defaults based on instance size; set max_connections according to memory but rely on pooling; configure autovacuum aggressively for busy tables; enable pg_stat_statements and log_min_duration_statement to find slow queries; and set timeouts such as statement_timeout and idle_in_transaction_session_timeout. Connections: use the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy or connectors with private IP, application-side pooling, or managed connection pooling to avoid exhaustion. Backups: enable automated backups with point-in-time recovery, test restores, and pick a retention policy. Maintenance: set maintenance windows and deny periods. Security: private IP, IAM database authentication, SSL enforcement, and CMEK if required. Observability: Query Insights for top queries, plans, and wait events, plus Cloud Monitoring alerts on CPU, memory, disk, connections, and replication lag. Regularly review index usage and bloat, and load test.`,
    f: ["What does Query Insights show you?","Why does disk size affect performance on Cloud SQL?"]
  },
  31: {
    a: `Cloud Spanner is Google's fully managed, horizontally scalable, strongly consistent relational database. It offers SQL, schemas, and ACID transactions with external consistency across rows, tables, and even regions, using TrueTime, with automatic sharding and replication, and availability SLAs up to 99.999 percent in multi-region configurations. You scale by adding nodes or processing units, with online schema changes and no downtime for maintenance. Choose it over Cloud SQL when you need one or more of: scale beyond what a single primary can handle, for write throughput or storage in the many terabytes, without manual sharding; global distribution with consistent transactions across regions; very high availability without managing failover; or continuous growth where you want to avoid re-architecting later. Cloud SQL, with PostgreSQL or MySQL, is the better choice for conventional applications of moderate size: it's cheaper, fully compatible with standard engines and extensions, easier to migrate to, and supports familiar tooling. Trade-offs of Spanner: higher cost, especially at small scale, since it has a minimum capacity; schema design requirements such as avoiding monotonically increasing primary keys to prevent hotspots, and using interleaved tables or foreign keys with locality in mind; some differences from standard SQL dialects, though a PostgreSQL interface is available; and migration effort. Evaluate the actual scale and availability requirements before committing.`,
    f: ["Why does Spanner cost more at small scale?","What is TrueTime used for?"]
  },
  32: {
    a: `A zero-downtime schema migration on a large table means never holding long locks or breaking running application code. Principles: use the expand and contract pattern in separate steps, so old and new application versions both work. Expand: add new columns as nullable or with a default that doesn't rewrite the table, which modern PostgreSQL supports instantly for constants; add new tables; create indexes concurrently with CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY in PostgreSQL, or use online DDL in MySQL with ALGORITHM=INPLACE. Add constraints in two steps, such as NOT VALID then VALIDATE CONSTRAINT, to avoid long locks. Migrate: deploy code that writes to both old and new structures, then backfill existing rows in small batches with pauses, monitoring replication lag and load, rather than one huge UPDATE. Switch reads to the new column, verify, then stop writing the old one. Contract: remove old columns or tables in a later release. For changes that can't be done in place, such as changing a column type, use online schema change tools: gh-ost or pt-online-schema-change for MySQL, pg_repack or a shadow-table approach in PostgreSQL, which copy data to a new table while tracking changes, then swap. Always set lock_timeout so the migration fails fast instead of queuing behind a long transaction and blocking everything.`,
    f: ["Why set a lock_timeout during migrations?","How do online schema change tools work?"]
  },
  33: {
    a: `Backups protect against data loss, and a backup you haven't restored is only a hope, so I treat restore testing as part of the job. Types: logical backups, like pg_dump or mysqldump, which are portable but slow for big databases and give a point-in-time snapshot only; physical backups, copies of the data files, such as pg_basebackup, pgBackRest, Percona XtraBackup, or cloud snapshots, which are faster to take and restore; and continuous archiving of WAL or binary logs for point-in-time recovery. Do it safely: take backups from a replica to avoid loading the primary, make sure they are consistent, encrypt them, store copies off-site and in a different account or region with immutability to resist ransomware, monitor success and size, and keep retention aligned with requirements. Restore regularly into a separate environment, verify data integrity, and time it. RPO, the recovery point objective, is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time; it determines backup frequency and whether you need continuous WAL archiving or synchronous replication. RTO, the recovery time objective, is the maximum acceptable downtime to restore service; it determines the approach: restoring a large backup could take hours, so low RTO needs standby replicas, faster snapshots, or automated failover. Define them with the business, document runbooks, and run disaster recovery drills.`,
    f: ["Why is replication not a backup?","How do RPO and RTO drive the design?"]
  },
  34: {
    a: `When a database disk is filling up, first assess how fast and what is growing, and buy time. Check space usage by component: data files, indexes, WAL or binary logs, temporary files, logs, and backups on the same volume. In PostgreSQL, find the largest tables and indexes using pg_total_relation_size and check pg_wal; a stuck replication slot or failing archive_command makes WAL pile up, and a long-running transaction can prevent vacuum, causing bloat. In MySQL, check binary log retention, relay logs, temp tables, and the slow log. Immediate relief without downtime: expand the volume online if the platform supports it, as cloud disks usually do, or enable automatic storage growth; drop an abandoned replication slot, fix archiving, purge old binary logs safely after confirming replicas have consumed them, rotate or compress logs, and delete unneeded temp files or old backups. Don't delete WAL or data files manually. Then address root causes: bloat, by tuning autovacuum and rebuilding with pg_repack; unneeded indexes; large tables where you can archive or purge old data, using partitioning to drop old partitions quickly; data growth that needs capacity planning; and runaway queries generating temp files. Prevent recurrence with alerts at 70, 80, and 90 percent, growth-rate forecasting, retention policies, and regular capacity reviews.`,
    f: ["Why can a replication slot fill the disk?","What would you never delete manually?"]
  },
  35: {
    a: `In Kubernetes, connection exhaustion is amplified by scale: each pod has its own pool, so total connections equal pods times pool size, and autoscaling or rolling updates multiply that, since surge pods briefly double connections. First, do the arithmetic: compare the maximum replicas times pool size, with extra for rollouts, against the database's max_connections. Diagnose with pg_stat_activity grouped by client address to see which workloads hold connections, and look for idle and idle-in-transaction sessions. Solutions: reduce per-pod pool sizes to what each pod needs, as it's usually much smaller than the default; use an external pooler such as PgBouncer, deployed as a Deployment or sidecar, or a managed one like Cloud SQL's pooling or RDS Proxy, so thousands of client connections multiplex onto a small number of server connections; set pool timeouts, max lifetime, and idle timeouts so connections are recycled and leaks detected. Ensure graceful shutdown closes connections on SIGTERM. Use connection retries with exponential backoff and jitter to avoid thundering herds at restart. Limit HPA maximum replicas relative to database capacity, and use PodDisruptionBudgets and slower rollout settings. Consider separating read traffic to replicas. Add application-level backpressure, such as queueing, circuit breakers and timeouts, rather than letting requests pile up. Monitor connection counts per service, pool wait times, and alert on utilisation.`,
    f: ["How do rolling updates multiply connections?","Where would you place PgBouncer in Kubernetes?"]
  },
  36: {
    a: `A partial index covers only the rows that satisfy a WHERE condition, instead of every row in the table. For example, CREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = 'pending'. The index is smaller, faster to scan, cheaper to maintain, since updates to rows outside the condition don't touch it, and it fits more easily in memory. It dramatically outperforms a full index when queries target a small, well-defined subset of a large table. Classic cases: a queue or job table where most rows are completed and queries look only for the unprocessed ones; soft-deleted data, indexing only where deleted_at IS NULL; a flag with skewed distribution, such as indexing only the rare true values; recent or active data; and enforcing uniqueness on a subset, such as unique email among active users, which a full unique index can't express. The query must include a predicate the planner can prove implies the index condition, so the condition in the query must match or be a stricter form of the index's WHERE clause; parameterised queries can prevent the proof if the predicate isn't a constant in the query. Downsides: it helps only the specific query shapes, adds an index to maintain and to reason about, and a mismatched predicate means it won't be used.`,
    f: ["What must a query contain for the planner to use a partial index?","How can a partial index enforce conditional uniqueness?"]
  },
  37: {
    a: `A Common Table Expression, introduced with WITH, defines a named temporary result set that a query can reference, improving readability by breaking complex logic into steps. They also enable recursive queries, such as traversing hierarchies or graphs, and let you reuse a subquery result several times in a statement, and chain data-modifying statements in PostgreSQL. Performance depends on the engine. Historically in PostgreSQL before version 12, CTEs were always materialised, acting as optimisation fences: the planner couldn't push predicates into them, which could make queries much slower, since the entire CTE was computed first. From version 12, non-recursive, side-effect-free CTEs referenced once are inlined by default, behaving like subqueries; you can force the old behaviour with MATERIALIZED or force inlining with NOT MATERIALIZED. Materialisation helps when the CTE is expensive and referenced multiple times, since it's computed once, or when you want to deliberately control the plan. It hurts when it materialises a huge set that a filter could have reduced, or prevents index use. MySQL 8 and others generally merge CTEs like derived tables. Guidelines: use CTEs for clarity, check the execution plan, avoid assuming they're free, and for hot paths test alternatives such as subqueries, temporary tables with indexes, or joins. Recursive CTEs need a termination condition to avoid endless loops.`,
    f: ["What changed for CTEs in PostgreSQL 12?","When is materialising a CTE beneficial?"]
  },
  38: {
    a: `Physical replication, PostgreSQL's streaming replication, ships the WAL, the byte-level changes to data pages, so the standby is an exact binary copy of the entire cluster. It replicates everything, all databases, tables, and indexes, requires the same major version and architecture, and the standby is read-only. It's ideal for high availability, failover, and read replicas. Logical replication decodes the WAL into logical changes, inserts, updates, and deletes at the row level, and publishes them for chosen tables using a publication-subscription model: the subscriber applies the changes as ordinary SQL operations. Differences and benefits: you can replicate selected tables rather than the whole cluster; the subscriber is a normal writable database, so it can have additional tables, different indexes, or even a different schema layout; it works across major PostgreSQL versions, enabling near-zero-downtime upgrades; it supports consolidating data from several sources and feeding other systems through logical decoding, as in change data capture with Debezium. Limitations: it doesn't replicate DDL or schema changes automatically, sequences, or large objects by default; tables generally need a primary key or replica identity; conflicts can occur on writable subscribers; performance overhead is higher; and it requires wal_level logical. Use physical replication for HA and read scaling, and logical replication for migrations, upgrades, selective replication, and data integration.`,
    f: ["How does logical replication enable major-version upgrades?","Why does logical replication need a replica identity?"]
  },
  39: {
    a: `Time-series data is append-heavy, queried by time ranges, and often aged out. In a relational database, the key techniques are as follows. Partition by time, for example daily or monthly range partitions, so queries prune to relevant partitions, inserts hit small hot partitions, and old data is dropped instantly by detaching or dropping partitions. Index carefully: a B-tree on timestamp plus the entity ID, or a BRIN index for naturally ordered data, which is tiny and effective for large append-only tables. Keep rows narrow with compact types. Cluster or order data physically by time so range scans are sequential. Batch inserts or use COPY for high ingest rates. Pre-aggregate: maintain rollup tables or continuous aggregates for hourly and daily summaries, so dashboards don't scan raw data, with raw data retained for a shorter period. Use retention policies, and compress or move older partitions to cheaper storage. Extensions like TimescaleDB automate this with hypertables, automatic chunking, native compression, continuous aggregates, and retention policies, while staying within PostgreSQL. Query tips: always filter on the time column, use time_bucket or date_trunc for aggregation, and avoid functions on the timestamp column in predicates. At very large scale or high cardinality, consider purpose-built stores like InfluxDB, ClickHouse, Prometheus, or Bigtable.`,
    f: ["Why is a BRIN index suited to time-series data?","How do rollups protect dashboard performance?"]
  },
  40: {
    a: `Database observability combines metrics, logs, and query-level insight. Metrics to watch: availability and connection counts relative to the limit; query throughput and latency percentiles; CPU, memory, disk I/O, IOPS and latency, and disk space; cache hit ratio and buffer pool behaviour; replication lag and status; lock waits, deadlocks, and long-running or idle-in-transaction sessions; autovacuum activity, dead tuples, bloat, and transaction ID age in PostgreSQL; checkpoint frequency, WAL generation, and temp file usage; and in MySQL, InnoDB metrics and slow query counts. Logs: slow query logs with a sensible threshold, error logs, deadlock logs, lock wait logging, autovacuum logs, and connection logs; use auto_explain to capture plans for slow queries. Query-level: pg_stat_statements, Performance Schema, or cloud tools like Query Insights and Performance Insights to find top queries by total time, with wait events. Tracing in the application connects slow requests to specific queries. Alerts should be actionable: connections above eighty percent, replication lag over a threshold, disk space and growth forecasts, rising query latency compared with baseline or SLO, failed backups, deadlock spikes, long transactions, and XID wraparound risk. Dashboards show the golden signals per database, with deployment markers. Add synthetic checks and regularly test restore and failover. Review dashboards and alerts after incidents and capacity-plan using trends.`,
    f: ["Which alerts would page someone?","How do wait events help diagnosis?"]
  },
  41: {
    a: `A materialised view stores the result of a query physically on disk, unlike a regular view, which is just a saved query that runs each time. Querying a materialised view is fast because the expensive joins and aggregations are already computed, and you can index it. It suits reporting, dashboards, and summary data where the underlying query is costly and slightly stale results are acceptable. In PostgreSQL, you create it with CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW and update it with REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW, which recomputes everything and locks reads during the refresh; REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY avoids blocking readers but requires a unique index and takes longer. PostgreSQL doesn't refresh them automatically or incrementally; you schedule refreshes with cron, pg_cron, or a job, or trigger them on data changes. Some databases, such as Oracle, Snowflake, and BigQuery, offer automatic or incremental refresh. The view goes stale between refreshes: it shows data as of the last refresh, so decide on acceptable staleness, set the refresh frequency to match, and expose the refresh time to users. Considerations: refresh cost grows with data size; refreshing too frequently can overload the database; storage overhead; and consistency, since the view may disagree with base tables. Alternatives include summary tables maintained incrementally by triggers or streaming jobs, and caching.`,
    f: ["What does REFRESH CONCURRENTLY require?","How do you handle acceptable staleness?"]
  },
  42: {
    a: `In MySQL, EXPLAIN shows how the optimiser plans to execute a query, with one row per table or subquery in the plan. Key columns. type describes the access method, from best to worst: system, const, eq_ref, ref, range, index, and ALL; ALL means a full table scan, which is the red flag on large tables, and index means a full index scan. possible_keys lists candidate indexes, while key shows the one actually chosen; a NULL key means none is used. key_len shows how much of a composite index is used, revealing whether all columns are being used. rows estimates how many rows will be examined, and filtered the percentage kept after conditions; multiplying across joined tables shows the total work. Extra gives crucial hints: Using index means a covering index; Using where; Using temporary and Using filesort signal temporary tables and extra sorting, which are costly on big data; Using index condition means index condition pushdown. Use EXPLAIN ANALYZE in MySQL 8 to get actual timings and row counts, and EXPLAIN FORMAT=TREE or JSON for more detail. Problems to find: full scans, no usable index, filesorts, large row estimates, and poor join order.`,
    f: ["What does type ALL indicate?","How does key_len help with composite indexes?"]
  },
  43: {
    a: `Both strategies handle concurrent modification of the same data. Pessimistic locking assumes conflicts are likely: it locks the row up front, for example SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, so other transactions block until the lock is released, which prevents conflicting updates entirely. It's simple to reason about and guarantees no lost updates, but holds locks, reducing concurrency, risks deadlocks, and hurts throughput, particularly if transactions are long or span user interaction. Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are rare: it reads without locks, then at update time checks that the data hasn't changed since it was read, typically using a version number or timestamp column: UPDATE items SET qty = ?, version = version + 1 WHERE id = ? AND version = ?. If zero rows are affected, someone else changed it, and the application retries or reports a conflict. It avoids holding locks, scales well for read-heavy workloads and long user think-times, and works across stateless services, but conflicts cause retries and wasted work, and under high contention it can thrash. Choose pessimistic when contention on the same rows is high, the cost of retry or failure is high, or operations are short and critical, like inventory decrement or ticket booking with strict guarantees, or queue processing with SKIP LOCKED.`,
    f: ["How does a version column detect conflicts?","When is SKIP LOCKED useful?"]
  },
  44: {
    a: `Database migrations in a CI/CD pipeline must be automated, versioned, tested, and safe for rolling deployments. Keep migration scripts in the repository with a tool such as Flyway, Liquibase, Alembic, or the framework's migrator, applied in order and tracked in a schema history table; never edit an applied migration. In CI, validate by running all migrations against a fresh database and against a copy or snapshot of production-like data, to catch errors and measure duration and locking; lint migrations for dangerous operations, using tools such as squawk or strong_migrations. Deploy as a distinct pipeline step that runs once, before the application rollout, using a Job or pipeline stage with a lock, not on each replica's startup. Design every change as backward compatible using expand and contract, since old and new application versions run simultaneously: add before use, backfill in batches, and remove only in a later release. Use lock_timeout, concurrent index creation, and online schema change tools for large tables. Use credentials with minimal necessary privilege. Take a backup or snapshot before risky releases, and plan rollback as roll forward or application rollback, since reverse migrations are risky.`,
    f: ["Why avoid editing an already applied migration?","What would a migration linter catch?"]
  },
  45: {
    a: `My methodology for a production database performance incident is structured: stabilise, observe, localise, fix, learn. First, confirm and scope the impact: which services and users, since when, and what changed recently, such as deployments, configuration, traffic, or failovers. If a recent change is a likely cause, roll it back early. Then look at database health in layers. Resources: CPU, memory, disk I/O and latency, network, and disk space; saturation indicates the bottleneck type. Connections: counts against limits, queued requests, idle-in-transaction sessions. Activity: what's running right now, from pg_stat_activity or the process list, sorted by duration; look for long-running queries, lock waits and blocking chains, and deadlocks. Query level: use pg_stat_statements or slow logs for the top queries by total time and any new or regressed ones, then EXPLAIN ANALYZE for the culprit to find missing indexes, plan changes, or stale statistics. Background work: autovacuum, checkpoints, backups, replication lag, and batch jobs. Mitigate fast with safe actions: kill the runaway or blocking queries, add or enable throttling, scale up or add replicas, redirect traffic, disable a heavy feature, or fix with an index created concurrently. Communicate status regularly. After recovery, verify against SLOs, write a blameless postmortem covering root cause and detection gaps, and add alerts, query limits, load tests, and capacity plans to prevent recurrence.`,
    f: ["What is the first question you ask about recent changes?","Which safe mitigations can you apply immediately?"]
  },
  46: {
    a: `A clustered index stores the table rows themselves in the order of the index key, so there can be only one per table. In MySQL InnoDB the primary key is the clustered index, and secondary indexes store the primary key value as their row pointer, which means a long primary key makes every secondary index larger and lookups need a second traversal. In SQL Server the clustered index is chosen explicitly. PostgreSQL has heap tables with all indexes non-clustered; the CLUSTER command reorders the table once but does not maintain it. Clustered ordering makes range scans on the key very fast because rows are adjacent, but random primary keys such as UUIDs cause page splits and fragmentation on inserts. I prefer compact, monotonically increasing keys for clustered storage, and I use secondary indexes, ideally covering ones, for other access paths.`,
    f: ["Why do long primary keys hurt InnoDB?","Why are random UUID keys a problem?"]
  },
  47: {
    a: `Cardinality is the number of distinct values in a column, and selectivity is how few rows a typical value returns relative to the table. An index on a high-selectivity column, like an email or order ID, lets the database jump to a handful of rows. An index on a boolean or a status column with three values returns a large part of the table, so the planner often prefers a sequential scan because random index lookups would cost more. The optimiser relies on statistics, histograms and most common values to estimate this, so stale statistics lead to bad plans; I run ANALYZE after bulk changes. Low-cardinality columns can still be useful as the leading column of a composite index paired with a selective one, or in a partial index that covers only the rare value, such as unprocessed rows. I judge an index by its effect on real query plans, not by theory.`,
    f: ["When is a low-cardinality index useful?","How do statistics affect the plan?"]
  },
  48: {
    a: `Every index slows writes, consumes storage and memory, and adds vacuum work, so unused ones are pure cost. In PostgreSQL I query pg_stat_user_indexes for indexes with zero or very few scans, and in MySQL the sys schema shows unused indexes. Statistics reset on restart or manual reset, so I observe over a complete business cycle, including month-end or seasonal jobs, before deciding. Duplicates and redundant indexes appear when one index is a prefix of another, for example an index on column A when another covers A and B. Unique and constraint-backing indexes must stay. Before dropping, I save the definition so it can be recreated, check replicas since usage may differ there, and drop with the concurrent option to avoid locks. In MySQL, I can make an index invisible first to test the effect without deleting it. Then I monitor query performance after the change.`,
    f: ["Why watch a full business cycle?","What is an invisible index in MySQL?"]
  },
  49: {
    a: `B-tree handles equality, range queries and ordering, and covers most needs. Hash indexes support equality only and are rarely preferred. GIN, the generalised inverted index, maps elements to rows and excels at containment queries on arrays, JSONB and full-text search, at the cost of slower writes. GiST supports overlap, geometry, range types and nearest-neighbour searches. SP-GiST helps with partitioned search spaces like prefixes. BRIN stores summaries of block ranges and is extremely small, ideal for huge append-only tables where values correlate with physical order, such as timestamps in a log table, but is useless if the data is randomly ordered. Choosing the right type depends on the operators in the query, so I verify with EXPLAIN that the index is used. Extensions add more, such as trigram indexes for fuzzy text matching.`,
    f: ["When does BRIN work well?","Why use GIN for JSONB?"]
  },
  50: {
    a: `An index-only scan returns results straight from the index when it contains every column the query needs, avoiding heap lookups. In PostgreSQL there is a catch from MVCC: the index does not hold row visibility information, so the executor must consult the visibility map to know whether all rows on a heap page are visible to everyone. If pages are not marked all-visible, because of recent updates or because vacuum has not run, it must still visit the heap, and the plan shows many heap fetches. To enable index-only scans I design covering indexes using the INCLUDE clause for payload columns, and I make sure autovacuum keeps up on frequently updated tables. The benefits are less I/O and better cache use, particularly for large tables, while the costs are bigger indexes and slower writes. I check the Heap Fetches figure in EXPLAIN ANALYZE to confirm the benefit.`,
    f: ["What does the visibility map record?","What does INCLUDE do?"]
  },
  51: {
    a: `With OFFSET, the database must produce and discard all skipped rows, so page one hundred thousand is slow and the cost grows linearly with depth. Results can also shift between requests when rows are inserted. Keyset pagination, also called the seek method, remembers the last row's sort key from the previous page and asks for rows after it using a where clause, which an index can satisfy directly, so every page costs about the same. The sort must be on a unique, indexed combination of columns, so I include a tie-breaker such as the primary key in both the order by and the comparison, using row-value comparison where supported. The trade-offs are that you cannot jump to an arbitrary page number and the cursor must be passed to clients. For APIs and infinite scroll it is the better choice; OFFSET remains acceptable for small tables or shallow pages.`,
    f: ["Why do you need a unique tie-breaker?","What do you lose with keyset pagination?"]
  },
  52: {
    a: `In MVCC databases like PostgreSQL, a count must check row visibility, so it scans the table or an index; on a table with hundreds of millions of rows that is expensive. I begin by asking whether an exact number is needed. For approximate totals such as a UI showing about 1.2 million results, the planner statistics give a cheap estimate. If an exact figure is needed, options include maintaining a counter table updated by triggers or application logic, though that adds write contention; using materialised or summary tables refreshed periodically; counting over a narrow indexed range such as the last day; or using an index-only scan on a small index with a well-vacuumed table. InnoDB also scans, though it can pick the smallest secondary index. For analytics, a columnar store or a cached aggregate is often a better design than counting on the transactional database.`,
    f: ["Why is count slow in PostgreSQL?","When are estimates acceptable?"]
  },
  53: {
    a: `UNION combines results and removes duplicates, which requires a sort or hash over the whole result and costs time and memory; UNION ALL simply concatenates, so when I know the sets are disjoint or duplicates are acceptable I use ALL. For IN versus EXISTS, modern optimisers often rewrite both into semi-joins and produce the same plan, but the semantic differences matter. EXISTS short-circuits at the first match and handles NULLs predictably. NOT IN behaves unexpectedly when the subquery returns NULL, because the comparison becomes unknown and no rows are returned, so I prefer NOT EXISTS or an anti-join. For large lists of constants, IN with a values list or a join against a temporary table can be better. As always, I check the actual plan with EXPLAIN on realistic data instead of assuming one form is faster.`,
    f: ["Why is NOT IN dangerous with NULLs?","When does UNION ALL matter for speed?"]
  },
  54: {
    a: `A pattern like name LIKE 'abc%' can use a B-tree index because it is a range scan on a prefix, although in PostgreSQL with non-C collations I need the text_pattern_ops operator class. A pattern with a leading wildcard, like '%abc', cannot use a normal index because there is no ordered starting point, so the database scans the entire table. For infix matching and fuzzy search, PostgreSQL's pg_trgm extension with a GIN or GiST index breaks text into trigrams and accelerates LIKE, ILIKE and similarity queries. For natural-language search with ranking, stemming and stop words, I use built-in full-text search with a tsvector column and a GIN index, and keep it updated with a generated column or trigger. MySQL offers FULLTEXT indexes. When requirements include relevance tuning, typo tolerance and facets at scale, a dedicated search engine such as Elasticsearch or OpenSearch is the right tool.`,
    f: ["Why can't '%abc' use a B-tree?","What is a trigram index?"]
  },
  55: {
    a: `Row-by-row inserts from an application pay a network round trip, a parse and often a commit for each row. The fastest approach in PostgreSQL is COPY, which streams data in bulk; MySQL has LOAD DATA. Where those are not possible, multi-row inserts or prepared statements batched in a single transaction cut overhead dramatically. For very large loads, I load into an unlogged or staging table, then transform and merge into the target, and consider dropping or deferring secondary indexes and constraints until after the load, rebuilding afterwards, which is faster than maintaining them for each row. Commit in sensible chunks, perhaps tens of thousands of rows, to limit lock time, transaction log size and replication lag. For bulk updates, I batch by primary key ranges with short transactions to avoid long locks and bloat. After large loads I run ANALYZE so the planner has fresh statistics.`,
    f: ["Why is COPY faster than inserts?","Why commit in chunks?"]
  },
  56: {
    a: `A prepared statement is parsed once and executed many times with bound parameters. This saves parse and sometimes planning time, and crucially separates code from data, which is the primary defence against SQL injection, so I always parameterise queries rather than concatenating strings. The performance subtlety is plan reuse. After several executions, PostgreSQL may switch to a generic plan that does not depend on the parameter value; when data is skewed, for example one customer has millions of rows and others few, the generic plan may be poor for some values. The setting plan_cache_mode lets me force custom plans per execution. With connection poolers in transaction mode, session-level prepared statements can break unless the pooler supports protocol-level prepared statements. In the application, ORMs and drivers often prepare automatically, so I check their configuration when diagnosing odd plan behaviour.`,
    f: ["What is a generic plan?","Why can transaction pooling break prepared statements?"]
  },
  57: {
    a: `Multi-version concurrency control lets readers and writers avoid blocking each other, because each transaction sees a snapshot. In PostgreSQL an update writes a new row version and marks the old one as dead for later transactions, and a delete only marks it. Dead versions stay in the table until vacuum can prove that no active transaction still needs them. If updates are frequent and vacuum cannot keep up, or a long-running transaction or a lagging replica with feedback holds back the cleanup horizon, the table and its indexes accumulate dead space, called bloat, which makes scans slower and wastes cache. Mitigation includes tuning autovacuum to be more aggressive for hot tables, avoiding long idle-in-transaction sessions, using fillfactor to enable heap-only tuple updates, and occasionally rebuilding with pg_repack or a concurrent reindex. Monitoring dead tuple ratios helps catch it early.`,
    f: ["What blocks vacuum from cleaning?","What is a HOT update?"]
  },
  58: {
    a: `PostgreSQL uses 32-bit transaction identifiers, compared in a circular fashion, so each row's age relative to the current transaction must stay below about two billion. Vacuum freezes old rows, marking them as visible to all future transactions. If freezing does not happen, the system eventually stops accepting writes to protect data from appearing to vanish, which is a severe outage that requires single-user-mode vacuuming. Causes include autovacuum being disabled or too slow for large tables, long-running transactions or abandoned replication slots holding the horizon back, and prepared transactions left open. Prevention is to monitor the age of datfrozenxid and per-table relfrozenxid and alert at moderate thresholds, keep autovacuum healthy with sufficient workers and cost limits, kill stuck old transactions and drop unused replication slots. Large tables may need tuned freeze settings and scheduled manual vacuums in quiet hours.`,
    f: ["What do abandoned replication slots cause?","What does freezing do?"]
  },
  59: {
    a: `The default trigger is a fraction of the table, twenty percent dead rows, which for a table with a billion rows means hundreds of millions of dead tuples before vacuum starts. For large or hot tables I set per-table storage parameters: a much smaller scale factor and a fixed threshold, so vacuum triggers earlier and does smaller, steadier work. If vacuum is running but too slowly, I raise the cost limit or reduce the cost delay so it does more work per cycle, and increase the number of autovacuum workers and maintenance memory when many tables need attention. I watch pg_stat_user_tables for dead tuple counts, last autovacuum times, and pg_stat_progress_vacuum for running jobs, and check that long transactions are not preventing cleanup. For insert-only tables, the insert threshold setting matters for freezing and visibility map updates. Tuning is iterative, guided by metrics.`,
    f: ["Why is the default scale factor bad for big tables?","How do you see vacuum progress?"]
  },
  60: {
    a: `Plain VACUUM marks space reusable but rarely returns it to the operating system, so bloated tables stay large. VACUUM FULL rewrites the entire table into a compact file but holds an exclusive lock for the duration, blocking reads and writes, which is unacceptable on live production tables. Alternatives work online: REINDEX CONCURRENTLY rebuilds an index while allowing normal operations, taking extra disk space and time; pg_repack creates a new copy of the table with triggers capturing changes, then swaps it in briefly under lock. These need free disk space about the size of the table and can add load and replication lag, so I run them in quiet periods and watch. Before repair, I confirm that bloat is real, using estimation queries or pgstattuple, and fix the cause, such as stuck transactions or insufficient autovacuum, otherwise it returns.`,
    f: ["Why avoid VACUUM FULL in production?","How do you measure bloat?"]
  },
  61: {
    a: `EXPLAIN with ANALYZE executes the query and shows actual timings and row counts, and the BUFFERS option adds how many pages were found in shared buffers, as hits, versus read from the operating system or disk. A plan node with a high read count shows where I/O is spent; with track_io_timing enabled I also see time waiting on I/O. I first compare estimated rows with actual rows at each node: a large mismatch means stale or insufficient statistics, correlated columns or a misestimate, which leads the planner into bad join orders or scan choices. I look for sequential scans on large tables where an index should work, nested loops with huge outer row counts, sorts or hashes spilling to disk, shown as temp blocks, and repeated loops. Because ANALYZE runs the statement, I wrap data-changing queries in a transaction that I roll back. Fixes include indexes, statistics targets, query rewrites and work memory changes.`,
    f: ["What do shared hit and read mean?","Why wrap DML in a rollback?"]
  },
  62: {
    a: `InnoDB writes changes to the redo log before the data pages are flushed, so after a crash it can replay committed changes; the setting innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit decides whether each commit forces the log to disk, with the default of one giving full durability, and zero or two trading some durability for speed. The undo log stores previous versions of rows, which lets transactions roll back and lets consistent reads see older snapshots, implementing MVCC; long transactions make undo grow and slow purge. The doublewrite buffer protects against partial page writes during a crash by writing pages first to a safe area before their final location. Understanding these explains common behaviours: commit latency is dominated by log fsync, large transactions inflate undo and redo, and a redo log that is too small causes aggressive flushing and stalls. Tuning involves log file size, flush method and I/O capacity.`,
    f: ["What does innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=1 mean?","Why do long transactions hurt?"]
  },
  63: {
    a: `At the default REPEATABLE READ level, InnoDB uses next-key locks, a record lock plus a lock on the gap before it, when scanning an index range with locking reads or updates, to prevent phantom rows. Two transactions can each lock a gap and then both try to insert into it; because gap locks do not conflict with each other but do block inserts, they wait on each other and deadlock. These situations appear around unique-key checks, range updates and delete-then-insert patterns. I diagnose with the latest detected deadlock section of the engine status or the deadlock logging option, which shows the statements and locks. Mitigations include keeping transactions short, accessing rows in a consistent order, using more selective indexes so fewer gaps are locked, switching to READ COMMITTED where phantom protection is not required, and adding retry logic in the application, since deadlocks are normal and recoverable.`,
    f: ["What does a gap lock protect against?","Why should apps retry on deadlock?"]
  },
  64: {
    a: `Statement-based replication logs SQL statements and is compact, but nondeterministic functions and some statements can produce different results on replicas. Row-based replication logs actual row changes, which is deterministic and safe, with larger logs for bulk updates. Mixed switches automatically. Today row format is the standard. GTIDs assign every transaction a globally unique identifier, so a replica tracks which transactions it has executed instead of binary log file names and positions. This makes failover and topology changes much easier: when promoting a replica or repointing others, auto-position figures out where to continue, avoiding manual position arithmetic and the errors it invites. GTIDs also help detect errant transactions that exist on a replica but not the source. I combine GTID with semi-synchronous or group replication for stronger durability and monitor replica lag and errors continuously.`,
    f: ["Why is row-based replication safer?","What is an errant transaction?"]
  },
  65: {
    a: `Altering a large table can lock it or take hours. Online schema change tools avoid that. They create a copy of the table with the new structure, copy rows in small chunks to limit load, and keep the copy in sync with ongoing changes, pt-online-schema-change using triggers and gh-ost reading the binary log, which avoids trigger overhead and allows throttling and pausing. At the end they swap the tables with a quick atomic rename. I run a dry-run first, check disk space for the copy, set throttling based on replica lag and server load, and schedule the cutover at a quiet time. Considerations include foreign keys, which complicate things, tables without primary keys, and a plan to abort cleanly. Newer MySQL versions support many in-place and instant alters natively, so I check the algorithm available before reaching for a tool.`,
    f: ["How does gh-ost avoid triggers?","When is a native instant ALTER enough?"]
  },
  66: {
    a: `Lag appears when the replica cannot apply changes as fast as the source produces them. Common causes are single-threaded apply of a very write-heavy workload, which I address with parallel replication using multiple workers; large transactions such as bulk deletes or alters that take as long to apply as to run and block everything behind them, which I fix by batching work into smaller transactions; replicas with slower disks or less memory than the source; heavy analytical reads on the replica competing for resources; and tables lacking a primary key or suitable index, which forces full scans for each row event under row-based replication. I measure lag with replica status and heartbeat tables, since seconds-behind-source can be misleading. Applications that read from replicas must tolerate lag or use read-your-writes routing, and I alert on lag thresholds.`,
    f: ["Why is a missing primary key harmful for replicas?","How do you handle read-after-write needs?"]
  },
  67: {
    a: `A database can only make progress on as many queries as it has CPU cores and I/O capacity; extra concurrent connections just add context switching, lock contention and memory use, so throughput drops when the pool is too large. A common starting formula is about twice the number of cores plus the number of effective disks, then I load test and adjust. The total across all application instances matters, so with many pods each configured with a pool of twenty, the database sees their sum. Therefore I size per-instance pools from a global budget, and use a server-side pooler such as PgBouncer or ProxySQL to multiplex many clients onto a few database connections. I set timeouts for acquiring connections, idle timeout, and maximum lifetime to recycle connections, and monitor wait time for connections in the application, which signals real saturation better than the pool size does.`,
    f: ["Why can a bigger pool be slower?","How does the total across instances matter?"]
  },
  68: {
    a: `PgBouncer sits between applications and PostgreSQL, accepting many client connections and mapping them to a small number of server connections. In session mode a client keeps a server connection until it disconnects, which is compatible with all features but gives limited multiplexing. In transaction mode the server connection is returned to the pool when the transaction ends, so thousands of mostly idle clients can share a few dozen server connections, which is the usual choice for web applications. The cost is that anything relying on session state does not work reliably: session-level advisory locks, temporary tables, SET commands that persist, LISTEN and NOTIFY, and in older versions protocol-level prepared statements. Statement mode releases after every statement and forbids multi-statement transactions. I monitor with SHOW POOLS and SHOW STATS, watch client wait times, and size default_pool_size to the database's capacity.`,
    f: ["What breaks in transaction mode?","How do you monitor PgBouncer?"]
  },
  69: {
    a: `A session that issued BEGIN and then stopped, perhaps because the application crashed, waited on an external call, or forgot to commit, stays idle in transaction. It keeps its locks, blocking other writers, and holds an old snapshot, which prevents vacuum from removing dead rows anywhere in the database, leading to bloat and eventually wraparound risk. I find these by querying pg_stat_activity for that state ordered by transaction age, and by alerting on the maximum age. The preventive control is idle_in_transaction_session_timeout, which terminates such sessions after a limit; I set a value appropriate to the application, maybe tens of seconds. In the application, I make transactions short, avoid making HTTP calls or waiting on users inside them, and make sure the framework releases connections in finally blocks. For immediate relief I terminate the oldest offender after confirming it is safe.`,
    f: ["Why does it block vacuum?","How do you prevent it?"]
  },
  70: {
    a: `When a database restarts or fails over, every application instance tries to reconnect at once, often with identical retry intervals, creating a thundering herd that can exhaust connection limits and CPU, slowing recovery or causing another failure. I mitigate it in layers. Clients retry with exponential backoff and random jitter, and their pools have bounded sizes and acquisition timeouts. A server-side pooler or proxy absorbs the reconnection burst by queuing clients while keeping the number of real server connections low. The database's maximum connections limit and reserved connections for administrators ensure I can still log in. Applications should fail fast and shed load or serve degraded responses instead of piling up requests. After failover, DNS or proxy endpoints should point to the new primary quickly, and caches and prepared statements should be re-established gradually. I rehearse failovers to see how the whole system behaves.`,
    f: ["Why add jitter to retries?","What reserved connections are for?"]
  },
  71: {
    a: `In PostgreSQL each client connection is a separate backend process with its own memory for caches and state, and queries can allocate work_mem for each sort or hash node, so worst-case memory is connections times work_mem times operations per query. Raising max_connections to several thousand without thought can cause out-of-memory kills, heavy context switching and slower snapshot computation. Typical advice is to keep max_connections moderate, a few hundred, and introduce a pooler for scale. I also keep a few connections reserved for superusers. If applications really need many concurrent sessions, I check whether they hold connections idle, which a pooler can fix. MySQL uses threads with lighter per-connection overhead but similar principles apply with per-thread buffers. When capacity is an issue, adding read replicas or vertical scale is better than allowing more simultaneous connections.`,
    f: ["How is worst-case memory computed?","Why use a pooler instead?"]
  },
  72: {
    a: `A replication slot guarantees that the primary keeps all WAL segments a consumer, such as a standby or a logical decoding client like a CDC tool, has not yet received. That prevents the replica from falling irrecoverably behind. The danger is that if the consumer disconnects or stops, the slot stays and the primary keeps accumulating WAL indefinitely, which can fill the disk and take the primary down. It can also hold back the cleanup horizon for logical slots. I monitor each slot's active status and retained WAL size, alert when they grow, and drop slots that belong to decommissioned consumers. Newer versions allow max_slot_wal_keep_size to cap retention, trading safety for the consumer by invalidating the slot rather than endangering the primary. Slots should be named and documented so owners are known.`,
    f: ["How can a slot fill the disk?","What does max_slot_wal_keep_size do?"]
  },
  73: {
    a: `Failover has to detect that the primary is really down, which is hard because network partitions can look like failures. A coordination service or a tool such as Patroni uses a distributed lock so only one node can be primary. Steps are: ensure the old primary is fenced so it cannot accept writes, promote the most up-to-date replica, repoint clients through a virtual IP, DNS, proxy or service endpoint, and reconfigure the other replicas to follow the new primary. Risks include split brain, where two nodes accept writes and data diverges; data loss with asynchronous replication, as committed transactions not yet shipped are lost, which synchronous replication or quorum commit reduces at a latency cost; and application behaviour, like cached connections or DNS TTLs delaying cutover. Afterwards the old primary must be rebuilt or rewound. I rehearse failovers regularly and measure recovery time.`,
    f: ["What is split brain?","How does synchronous replication help?"]
  },
  74: {
    a: `Replicas offload read traffic but reflect the primary with a delay, so correctness depends on which reads can tolerate staleness. Reports, search pages and dashboards usually can; a user who just changed a setting and then reloads the page usually cannot. Strategies include routing a user's reads to the primary for a short window after they write, tracking the log position of the last write and only reading from replicas that have caught up to it, or using sticky sessions. Monitoring replica lag in time and bytes lets the router stop sending traffic to replicas beyond a threshold and fall back to the primary. Proxies like ProxySQL or application-level routing implement this. Also consider that replicas can have query conflicts with replay, and that heavy analytical queries on a replica can cause lag, so I separate reporting replicas from those serving user traffic.`,
    f: ["What is read-your-writes?","Why separate analytics replicas?"]
  },
  75: {
    a: `A physical backup copies the database's files, as a base backup, together with the write-ahead log, enabling fast restore of the whole cluster and point-in-time recovery to a chosen moment. It is version- and architecture-specific. A logical backup exports schema and data as SQL or a custom format, like pg_dump or mysqldump; it is portable across versions and platforms, supports restoring individual tables, and is useful for migrations and upgrades, but is slower to restore on large databases and gives a single point in time. In production I use physical backups with continuous WAL or binary log archiving as the primary disaster recovery method, using tools like pgBackRest or managed snapshots, and add periodic logical dumps for portability and small-scope recovery. All backups need encryption, offsite copies and regularly tested restores that measure real recovery time.`,
    f: ["What does PITR need?","Why test restores?"]
  },
  76: {
    a: `Change data capture reads the database's transaction log to emit every insert, update and delete as an ordered event stream. Instead of polling tables or having the application write to both the database and a message queue, which risks inconsistency, CDC derives events from the committed log. In PostgreSQL, logical decoding exposes changes through a replication slot, and tools like Debezium turn them into messages on Kafka; MySQL tools read the binary log. Use cases are keeping search indexes and caches in sync, feeding analytics warehouses, building event-driven integrations and migrations with minimal downtime. Operational concerns include the slot retaining WAL if the consumer stalls, schema evolution needing coordinated handling, ordering and exactly-once semantics at the consumer, initial snapshots of existing data, and handling of large transactions. Monitoring consumer lag and slot size is essential.`,
    f: ["Why is CDC better than dual writes?","What are the operational risks?"]
  },
  77: {
    a: `Data types affect storage size, memory use, index size and speed. Smaller types mean more rows per page and better cache efficiency, so I choose integer sizes to fit the realistic range but with growth in mind, because changing an integer primary key to bigint later on a huge table is painful. I store numbers as numeric types, not strings, dates as proper date or timestamp types, and use fixed-point decimals for money rather than floating point. Join and foreign key columns must have identical types, otherwise implicit casts can prevent index usage. Text columns of unbounded size should avoid being in frequently scanned wide tables; large values are stored out of line. Enumerations or small lookup tables can replace repeated long strings. UUIDs are convenient but larger and random, so ordered variants help index locality. Good type discipline early prevents expensive migrations.`,
    f: ["Why must join columns have identical types?","Why use numeric for money?"]
  },
  78: {
    a: `Auto-increment integers are small, fast to compare, ordered so inserts append to the right edge of the B-tree, and friendly to caches. Their downsides are exposure of record counts and guessable identifiers if exposed in URLs, and coordination difficulties when generating IDs across multiple writers or shards. UUIDs can be generated anywhere without coordination, are hard to guess and simplify merging data from different systems, but at sixteen bytes they enlarge every index referencing them, and random version-four values scatter inserts across the index, causing page splits, poor cache locality and write amplification. Time-ordered variants such as UUIDv7 or ULID combine uniqueness with roughly sequential inserts and mitigate most of that cost. In clustered storage like InnoDB the effect of random keys is especially strong. I choose integers for internal single-database tables and time-ordered UUIDs when distributed generation or opaque identifiers are required.`,
    f: ["Why are random UUIDs slow to insert?","What is UUIDv7?"]
  },
  79: {
    a: `There are three common models. A shared schema stores all tenants in the same tables with a tenant identifier column: it is the most efficient and simplest to operate, but needs strict query discipline, often enforced with row-level security, and noisy neighbours can affect each other. Schema-per-tenant gives stronger separation and per-tenant customisation but becomes hard to manage with thousands of tenants because of catalog size and migrations. Database-per-tenant gives maximum isolation and easy per-tenant backup or placement, at the highest cost. For shared schema performance, I make tenant_id the leading column of primary keys and relevant indexes so queries touch only one tenant's contiguous data, and consider partitioning or sharding by tenant for very large ones. Large tenants may be moved to dedicated infrastructure. Rate limits and resource quotas protect against noisy neighbours.`,
    f: ["Why lead with tenant_id?","How does row-level security help?"]
  },
  80: {
    a: `JSONB offers flexibility to store attributes that vary between rows or change often without schema migrations, and PostgreSQL can index it with GIN and query it with containment and path operators. But overusing it loses relational advantages: constraints, foreign keys, type checking, straightforward joins, efficient statistics for the planner, and compact storage. Updating a small field rewrites the whole JSON value. My rule is that core entities and fields used in joins, filtering, sorting or aggregation belong in proper columns, while optional metadata, third-party payloads and rarely queried attributes can live in JSONB. If a JSON field becomes hot, I promote it to a real column, possibly a generated column from the JSON with its own index. Validation can be enforced with check constraints or application schemas. The hybrid approach gives flexibility where it is valuable and rigor elsewhere.`,
    f: ["What do you lose with JSONB?","What is a generated column?"]
  },
  81: {
    a: `Foreign keys enforce that referenced rows exist, preventing orphans, which is far cheaper than discovering corrupt data later. The cost is that inserts and updates to the child table must verify the parent, and deletes or key updates on the parent must check or cascade to children. PostgreSQL does not automatically index the referencing column, so without an index those parent-side checks scan the child table and can cause locking and slow deletes; I create indexes on foreign key columns. On extremely high-throughput systems, some teams drop foreign keys for sharded or very large tables and enforce integrity in application code, accepting the risk; in distributed databases cross-shard constraints may not be available. My default is to keep them, because integrity bugs are expensive, and remove them only where measurements show a real problem and compensating controls exist.`,
    f: ["Why index the referencing column?","When might you drop foreign keys?"]
  },
  82: {
    a: `DynamoDB distributes items across partitions by hashing the partition key, and each partition has throughput limits. If many requests target the same key value, for example a popular product or a date used as the key for all current writes, that partition throttles even if the table has spare capacity overall. Avoiding hot partitions starts with choosing a high-cardinality partition key that spreads access evenly, like user or device identifiers. For unavoidable hot keys, I use write sharding, appending a random or calculated suffix to the key and reading with a scatter-gather, or aggregating in a buffer before writing. Adaptive capacity and on-demand mode help absorb uneven loads but are not a fix for extreme skew. Caching hot reads in DAX or a cache layer also helps. Data modelling in DynamoDB begins with the access patterns, then keys and secondary indexes are designed to serve them.`,
    f: ["What is write sharding?","Why start from access patterns?"]
  },
  83: {
    a: `Once Redis reaches maxmemory, its eviction policy decides what happens. Noeviction rejects writes with errors, appropriate when Redis is a primary data store and losing keys is unacceptable. Allkeys-lru evicts the least recently used keys among all keys, a good default for a cache, and allkeys-lfu favours frequency of use. The volatile policies only consider keys with an expiry set, so if few keys have TTLs they may find nothing to evict and fail writes. Volatile-ttl evicts those expiring soonest. Random policies exist but are rarely best. I choose based on role: cache means allkeys-lru or lfu with TTLs on entries, while queues or session stores need careful capacity planning and noeviction with alerts. I monitor memory usage, evicted keys, hit ratio and fragmentation, and size maxmemory below the machine's RAM to leave headroom for forks and buffers.`,
    f: ["Why can volatile policies fail?","What should you monitor in Redis?"]
  },
  84: {
    a: `A cache stampede occurs when a popular key expires and many requests simultaneously miss and hit the database to rebuild it. Defences include a single-flight lock so only one request recomputes while others wait or serve the stale value, adding random jitter to expiry times so keys do not all expire together, and refreshing early or serving stale data while revalidating in the background. For consistency, the common pattern is cache-aside: read from the cache, on a miss load from the database and populate; on writes update the database and then delete the cache entry, which is safer than updating it because of races. There is always a short window of inconsistency, so I define acceptable staleness, set TTLs as a safety net, and for strict needs use write-through or event-based invalidation from change data capture. I monitor hit ratio and database load.`,
    f: ["What is cache-aside?","Why delete instead of update on write?"]
  },
  85: {
    a: `MongoDB queries without a supporting index perform collection scans, so I create indexes matching query patterns. For compound indexes the guideline is equality fields first, then sort fields, then range fields, so the index serves filtering and ordering together. Explain with execution statistics shows documents examined versus returned; a large ratio indicates inefficiency. Covered queries, where the projection is satisfied by the index alone, avoid fetching documents. In aggregation pipelines, stage order matters: put match and sort stages early so they can use indexes, project only required fields, and limit before expensive stages like lookup or group, because later stages cannot use indexes in most cases and large groups may spill to disk. I avoid unbounded array growth in documents, watch the working set against RAM, and use the profiler to find slow operations. Shard keys need high cardinality and even distribution.`,
    f: ["What is the equality-sort-range rule?","What is a covered query?"]
  },
  86: {
    a: `Distributed stores replicate data across nodes, and consistency describes what a read can see after a write. Eventual consistency guarantees replicas converge but a read may return stale data briefly; it offers the best availability and latency. Strong consistency ensures reads reflect the latest committed write, at the cost of higher latency and reduced availability during partitions. Many systems let me tune per operation. In quorum-based systems like Cassandra with N replicas, writing to W and reading from R replicas with R plus W greater than N guarantees overlap and so the read sees the latest write. DynamoDB offers eventually consistent reads by default and strongly consistent reads at higher cost, and transactions when needed. I choose strong consistency for things like balances and inventory, and eventual for feeds and counters, always designing around the CAP trade-offs and the business impact of stale reads.`,
    f: ["What does R + W > N guarantee?","When is eventual consistency fine?"]
  },
  87: {
    a: `People often confuse high availability with read scaling. A Multi-AZ or regional HA configuration keeps a synchronously replicated standby in another zone and promotes it on failure; in the classic model the standby does not serve traffic, so it adds resilience but not capacity. Read replicas replicate asynchronously and serve read queries, which scales reads but can lag and are not a guaranteed failover target unless promoted deliberately. Many managed services now offer variants that combine both. I plan for failover time, typically one to two minutes, with DNS endpoint changes, so applications must retry and reconnect, and I test by forcing a failover. Maintenance windows, minor version upgrades and storage scaling also can cause brief interruptions. I monitor replica lag, CPU, free storage and connections, and put cross-region replicas or backups in place for disaster recovery.`,
    f: ["Does Multi-AZ increase read capacity?","How do you test failover?"]
  },
  88: {
    a: `Database performance on cloud storage depends on IOPS, throughput and latency limits, which vary by volume type and size. General-purpose volumes often provide a baseline proportional to size plus burst credits; once credits are exhausted, performance falls to the baseline and latency spikes, a common cause of mysterious slowdowns after a busy period. I watch metrics such as read and write latency, disk queue depth, IOPS against the provisioned limit and burst balance. If the workload sustains high I/O, I move to provisioned IOPS storage or increase volume size to raise the baseline. Memory matters too: a larger buffer cache reduces physical reads. I also check for instance-level throughput caps, which can be the limit rather than the disk, and enable storage autoscaling with a maximum to avoid running out of space. Query and index optimisation usually reduces I/O more cheaply than buying it.`,
    f: ["What happens when burst credits run out?","Why can the instance limit I/O?"]
  },
  89: {
    a: `In standard engines each replica has its own copy of the data and replays the log. Aurora instead separates the database engine from a purpose-built distributed storage service that keeps six copies of the data across three availability zones and handles log application itself. Writers send log records, not full pages, to storage, reducing I/O. Read replicas attach to the same shared storage volume, so they need not replay data themselves; replica lag is typically milliseconds, and failover to a replica takes tens of seconds because there is no data copying. Storage grows automatically, and backups and snapshots are continuous and low impact. Trade-offs include cost, some vendor lock-in, and behaviour differences from stock engines, for example in extensions or tuning parameters. I still need good schema and query design, and I use cluster endpoints for writes and reader endpoints for load-balanced reads.`,
    f: ["Why is Aurora replica lag so low?","What are the trade-offs of Aurora?"]
  },
  90: {
    a: `Managed databases hide the operating system but expose rich telemetry. I track the basics: CPU, memory, free storage, connections, IOPS, throughput and latency, plus replication lag and replica health. Performance Insights or Query Insights show database load broken down by wait events and top SQL, which is the fastest way to see what is consuming time. Enhanced monitoring gives operating system metrics at fine granularity. Logs, including slow query logs and error logs, are exported to the cloud logging service for search and alerting. Alerts focus on conditions that affect users or will soon: storage nearly full, connection count near the limit, sustained high load, replica lag, failed backups, and failover events. I also review recommendations from the provider, schedule maintenance deliberately, and keep dashboards that combine database metrics with application latency so correlation is quick during incidents.`,
    f: ["What does Performance Insights show?","Which alerts matter most?"]
  },
  91: {
    a: `I begin by reading release notes for breaking changes, and testing the application against the new version in a staging environment restored from a recent production backup, including extensions, drivers, query plans and performance. In-place upgrades with pg_upgrade in link mode are fast but need downtime for the process and a tested rollback, such as keeping the old data directory or a snapshot. For near-zero downtime I build a new cluster on the target version, keep it in sync by logical replication, or use the cloud provider's blue-green deployment feature, then switch traffic after verifying data and performance, and keep the old cluster for a while as a fallback, with reverse replication if feasible. After the upgrade I run ANALYZE because statistics may not carry over, check query regressions, and monitor closely. Communication, a maintenance window and a clear go or no-go checklist are part of the plan.`,
    f: ["Why run ANALYZE after upgrading?","How does blue-green help?"]
  },
  92: {
    a: `I start by establishing the pattern: are spikes periodic, tied to deploys, batch jobs, or specific queries? Then I look at what sessions are waiting for, using wait events in pg_stat_activity or the Performance Schema in MySQL, which immediately separates lock waits, I/O waits and CPU. Periodic spikes often come from checkpoints flushing many dirty pages, autovacuum or autoanalyze jobs, backups, cron jobs or cache eviction after a deploy. Sudden plan changes after statistics updates or data growth can turn a fast query into a slow one; I compare plans with the pg_stat_statements history. Lock contention from long transactions or schema changes causes queues. I also check infrastructure: burst credits, throttling by the cloud provider, noisy neighbours, and connection storms. With the cause identified, I fix it specifically, for example spreading checkpoints, adjusting vacuum, adding an index, or limiting batch concurrency.`,
    f: ["What are wait events?","What causes periodic spikes?"]
  },
  93: {
    a: `I enable slow query logging with a sensible threshold such as a few hundred milliseconds, and include enough context like duration, rows and the statement, being careful about sensitive literals in logs. Individual log lines are useful but aggregation is better. PostgreSQL's pg_stat_statements normalises queries and tracks calls, total and mean time, rows and buffer usage; pgBadger analyses logs into reports; MySQL has the slow log with pt-query-digest or the Performance Schema. I rank queries by total time consumed, calls times mean time, because a moderately slow query called a million times hurts more than one very slow report. For each top query I examine the plan, index usage, row estimates and call patterns, such as N+1 queries from the application, and fix by indexing, rewriting, caching or batching. After changes, I compare before and after metrics and keep the monitoring so regressions are visible.`,
    f: ["Why rank by total time?","What does pg_stat_statements normalise?"]
  },
  94: {
    a: `I collect time-series for database size, growth by table, queries per second, latency percentiles, CPU, memory, I/O and connections, and look at daily and weekly peaks as well as the growth trend. Memory sizing aims for the hot working set and indexes to fit in cache, since misses go to disk. Storage planning includes data, indexes, WAL or binary logs, temporary files, backups and bloat, plus headroom for maintenance operations like rebuilding indexes that need extra space. I project growth with business forecasts, such as expected user numbers or new features, add a safety margin, and decide in advance triggers for action: when CPU stays above sixty percent at peak, or storage reaches seventy percent. Scaling options are vertical increases, read replicas, caching, archiving old data, partitioning, or sharding, each with different effort. I load test before big events and review capacity quarterly.`,
    f: ["What should fit in memory?","What triggers a scaling action?"]
  },
  95: {
    a: `Large deletes create huge transactions, lock contention, bloat, long replication lag and heavy log volume. The best design is time-based partitioning: old data lives in its own partition, which I detach and archive or drop instantly with negligible overhead. If the table is not partitioned, I delete in small batches of a few thousand rows ordered by an indexed key, committing each batch, pausing between them to let replicas keep up and vacuum clean, and monitoring lock waits and lag, with the ability to stop at any time. Before deleting, I archive to cheaper storage like object storage in a columnar format, so compliance and analytical needs are met. Afterwards, I check for bloat and reclaim space if needed. Retention rules should be defined with the business and automated, and verified by tests, so data growth is controlled continuously and not through emergency cleanups.`,
    f: ["Why is dropping a partition better than deleting?","How do you throttle batch deletes?"]
  },
  96: {
    a: `When many sessions are waiting, the real problem is usually one blocker at the head of the chain. I find it using pg_blocking_pids or the sys schema's lock waits in MySQL, and look at what the blocker is doing, who owns it and how long it has run. If it is a long idle transaction or an ad hoc query, I try a gentle cancel of the query first, and terminate the session only if needed, aware that rolling back a large transaction can itself take time and keep locks. If it is a schema change waiting for a lock and queuing everything behind it, cancelling it clears the queue. Afterwards I address causes: set statement timeout, lock timeout and idle-in-transaction timeout; run DDL with a short lock timeout and retries; make application transactions short; and review the offending query for indexing or batching. A runbook with these queries speeds up the response.`,
    f: ["Why cancel before terminate?","How does lock_timeout help DDL?"]
  },
  97: {
    a: `A useful database load test needs three realism points. The data must be production-sized and similarly distributed, because planner choices and cache behaviour change with scale and skew; restoring a masked copy of production is ideal. The workload must reflect real query mix, concurrency and think time, ideally by replaying captured traffic or by modelling the main transactions with a tool like pgbench, sysbench or a custom harness. The environment should match production instance types, storage and configuration, and be isolated so nothing touches live data. I ramp load gradually to find the knee where latency rises, record throughput, latency percentiles, error rates, and resource usage like CPU, I/O, locks and replication lag, and repeat after tuning to confirm improvements. Also test failure scenarios such as failover and replica loss under load. Results feed capacity plans and go or no-go decisions.`,
    f: ["Why does data skew matter in tests?","What is the knee in a load curve?"]
  }
};
